From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jean-Michael Celerier <jmcelerier@sat.qc.ca>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:11:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] rhi: add a write serial to QRhiBuffer

A consumer that mirrors or derives data from a buffer it does not own
(a copy into another buffer, an acceleration structure, an accumulated
image) has no way to find out if the content of that buffer changed:
the QRhiBuffer is the same before and after a write. It has to redo
its work in every frame, or rely on every producer reporting its
writes through a side channel.

QRhi sees every write that goes through it. Add
QRhiBuffer::writeSerial(), a value that changes whenever an operation
that defines or may change the content of the buffer is recorded:

- create(),
- a dynamic update or static upload of a resource update batch, when
  the batch is committed,
- beginFullDynamicBufferUpdateForCurrentFrame() and
  fullDynamicBufferUpdateForCurrentFrame(),
- setShaderResources() with the buffer in a BufferStore or
  BufferLoadStore binding, in render and compute passes.

Readbacks and BufferLoad bindings, and the vertex, index, indirect and
uniform usages do not change it. The values come from the process-wide
counter that already provides the resource ids, so a serial is never
repeated by another buffer, nor by the same QRhiBuffer after create():
comparing the value is enough even when a buffer is destroyed and
another one is allocated at the same address.

Writes QRhi cannot see (native commands on nativeBuffer() between
beginExternal() and endExternal()) are reported with the new
QRhiBuffer::noteExternalWrite().

The serial is 0 on a backend that does not maintain it; noteExternalWrite()
does nothing then. This change implements it for Vulkan, OpenGL and the
Null backend, with an autotest (tst_QRhi::bufferWriteSerial).
